/// Chapter 28 of the guide, written from md-docs/recipes/i18n.md by
/// scripts/docsite/guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page I18nGuide(path: "/docs/guide/i18n", title: "Internationalisation", description: "Translation files, t() with placeholders and plurals, choosing and switching the locale, right-to-left, and locale-aware formatting.", layout: DocsShell("Internationalisation", chapter: t("nav.group.content"), blurb: "Keep one JSON file per locale, read it with t(), and switch the whole site's language — and its direction — at once.", source: "md-docs/recipes/i18n.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "set-up-the-translation-files", label: "Set up the translation files"), Anchor(id: "start-a-project-translated", label: "Start a project translated"), Anchor(id: "translate-text-with-t", label: "Translate text with t()"), Anchor(id: "translate-by-the-words-as-written", label: "Translate by the words as written"), Anchor(id: "switch-the-locale", label: "Switch the locale"), Anchor(id: "choose-the-locale-a-page-opens-in", label: "Choose the locale a page opens in"), Anchor(id: "a-page-in-its-own-language", label: "A page in its own language"), Anchor(id: "format-numbers-and-dates-for-the-locale", label: "Format numbers and dates for the locale"), Anchor(id: "translate-long-form-text", label: "Translate long-form text"), Anchor(id: "check-what-each-locale-lacks", label: "Check what each locale lacks"), Anchor(id: "checklist", label: "Checklist")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/content", prevLabel: t("ch.27"), nextTo: "/docs/guide/testing", nextLabel: t("ch.29"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter shows you how to make your site speak several languages. You keep its text in one JSON file per *locale* — a language, and optionally a region, named by a tag such as `en`, `ar` or `pt-BR` — and read it with `t(\"key\")`. Switching the locale redraws every translated string, turns the document right to left where the language reads that way, and changes how `format` and `ago` write numbers and dates.\n\nIn this chapter you will:\n\n- set up the translation files, or start a project already translated;\n- translate text with `t()`, with placeholders and plural forms;\n- translate a project by the words it is written in;\n- switch the locale, and choose the one a page opens in;\n- format numbers and dates for the reader's locale;\n- check what each locale lacks.")

        Heading("Set up the translation files", id: "set-up-the-translation-files").h2

        Markdown("To translate a site, list its locales in `webfluent.app.json` under `i18n`. `default_locale` names the language the site is written in, and `dir` the directory that holds the files:")

        CodeBlock(#"{
  "i18n": {
    "default_locale": "en",
    "locales": ["en", "ar", "es"],
    "dir": "src/translations"
  }
}"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("Then put one file per locale in that directory — `en.json`, `ar.json`, `es.json`. Each is a flat map of keys to messages; by convention a key has dots in it to group related messages. This is `src/translations/en.json`:")

        CodeBlock(#"{
  "nav.home": "Home",
  "nav.about": "About",
  "welcome": "Welcome, {name}!",
  "items.one": "{count} item",
  "items.other": "{count} items",
  "actions.save": "Save"
}"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("And this is `src/translations/ar.json`, with the six plural forms Arabic uses:")

        CodeBlock(#"{
  "nav.home": "الرئيسية",
  "nav.about": "حول",
  "welcome": "أهلاً، {name}!",
  "items.zero": "لا عناصر",
  "items.one": "عنصر واحد",
  "items.two": "عنصران",
  "items.few": "{count} عناصر",
  "items.many": "{count} عنصراً",
  "items.other": "{count} عنصر",
  "actions.save": "حفظ"
}"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("A locale with no file, and a key one locale has and another lacks, are build warnings (`I03`). At run time a key missing from the reader's locale falls back to the default locale's message, then to the key itself.")

        Heading("Start a project translated", id: "start-a-project-translated").h2

        Markdown("To begin a new project in several languages, pass `--locales` to `wf init`, with the default locale first:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf init my-app --locales en,ar")
            ])

        Markdown("This works for the `spa` and `static` templates. The new project has:\n\n- the `i18n` config above, and `meta.lang` set to the default locale;\n- every visible string of the starter passed through `t(\"…\")`;\n- a language switch with one entry per locale;\n- a file for each locale in `src/translations/`. The default locale's holds the starter's English, which its pages and tests are written in. Arabic holds the starter's Arabic. Any other locale starts as a copy of the English, for you to translate.\n\nEach locale is a language tag (`en`, `ar`, `pt-BR`). `--locales ar,en` makes Arabic the default, so the page is right to left from the first paint. From Rust, the same is `webfluent::init::create_with(dir, name, &Options \{ template, locales \})`.")

        Heading("Translate text with t()", id: "translate-text-with-t").h2

        Markdown("To show a message in the reader's language, call `t(\"key\")` wherever a string goes. Pass a map as the second argument to fill placeholders and to pick a plural form:")

        CodeBlock(#"type User { name: String }

page Home(path: "/", title: "Home", description: "The home page.") {
    state user = User(name: "Sam")
    state items: [String] = ["a", "b", "c"]
    Heading(t("welcome", { name: user.name })).h1
    Text(t("items", { count: items.length }))
    Button(t("actions.save")).primary
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("In English this page reads **Welcome, Sam!**, **3 items** and a **Save** button; in Arabic, **أهلاً، Sam!**, **3 عناصر** and **حفظ**.\n\nHow `t()` behaves:\n\n- **It follows the locale.** `t(\"key\")` is the message for the current locale, and every call is evaluated again when the locale changes.\n- **It fills placeholders.** `t(\"key\", \{ name: value \})` replaces each `\{name\}` in the message with the map's value.\n- **A `count` picks the plural form.** The form comes from the locale's CLDR plural rules, which the browser's `Intl.PluralRules` follows too: Arabic has `zero`, `one`, `two`, `few`, `many` and `other`; English has `one` and `other`; Japanese has only `other`. A count of exactly 0 uses `key.zero` wherever the file has one, in any language, so English can say \"No files\" rather than \"0 files\". Without the form it wants, `t()` falls back to `key.other`, then to `key`.\n- **A key's forms all come from one language** — the reader's, when its file has the key at all, otherwise the default locale's. It never mixes one form from each.\n- **The pre-rendered page is already translated.** The build resolves `t` for the default locale when it pre-renders a page, by the same rules, so the HTML is in the default language, its counts in that language's forms.")

        Callout("Never build a sentence from pieces (`t(\"greeting\") + name`). Word order differs between languages. Put a placeholder in the message, so a translator can move it.", kind: .warning)

        Heading("Translate by the words as written", id: "translate-by-the-words-as-written").h2

        Markdown("To translate a project that was written in one language from the start, without inventing keys, set `\"literals\": true` in its `i18n` config. Every string a reader sees is then looked up in the reader's table by its text as written — an element's text, and props such as `label:`, `title:`, `placeholder:`, `aria-label:` and `alt:`. The Arabic table maps the English words to their translation:")

        CodeBlock(#"{ "Problems": "المشاكل", "{count} missing": "{count} مفقودة" }"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("The page keeps its English words:")

        CodeBlock(#"Heading("Problems").h2          // المشاكل, for a reader in ar
Text("{l.count} missing")       // one key, `{count} missing`"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("How the text becomes a key:\n\n- **A splice of a plain path is a placeholder.** It is named for the path's last part, so `\"\{l.count\} missing\"` is the key `\{count\} missing`. A splice of anything more, such as `\"\{n + 1\} more\"`, keeps the text as written.\n- **Each branch of an `if` is its own key** when the text chooses between them.\n- **A value shown by its name** — a `derived` or `state` — is looked up only when its name says it is words, such as `countText` or `emptyLabel`. Shown words that neither rule covers draw an `I05` warning.\n- **What is not words is left alone:** an icon's name, `Code(…)`, and a component named `Kbd`.\n- **A list of words is translated word by word.** A `labels:` prop, which holds one word for each option of a control whose options are values, is looked up item by item: `Segmented(options: [\"2\", \"4\"], labels: [\"2 spaces\", \"4 spaces\"])` on your own `Segmented` component.\n\nThe default locale's messages are the words themselves, so its file may be empty. `wf translations` counts each string as a key in use, and lists what another locale has not translated as missing.")

        Heading("Count by the number in the text", id: "count-by-the-number-in-the-text").h3

        Markdown("A literal with one number spliced into it picks its plural form by that number. The key's plural forms are keys too, as for `count`:")

        CodeBlock(#"{
  "{files} files.zero": "لا توجد ملفات",
  "{files} files.one": "ملف واحد",
  "{files} files.two": "ملفان",
  "{files} files.few": "{files} ملفات",
  "{files} files.many": "{files} ملفًا",
  "{files} files.other": "{files} ملف"
}"#, title: "json")

        CodeBlock(#"Text("{files} files")           // 11 ملفًا, for a reader in ar"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The default locale gives its forms the same way — `\"\{files\} files.one\": \"\{files\} file\"` — where its words differ by count. With two numbers in the text, neither is taken as the count: the message is looked up as written.")

        Heading("Switch the locale", id: "switch-the-locale").h2

        Markdown("To let the reader change language, call `setLocale(code)`. This app puts a language picker in the navigation bar:")

        CodeBlock(#"app {
    state chosen = "en"
    Navbar {
        Navbar.Brand { Link("i18n", to: "/") }
        Navbar.Links {
            Link(t("nav.home"), to: "/")
            Link(t("nav.about"), to: "/about")
        }
        Navbar.Actions {
            Select(bind: chosen, label: t("nav.language")) {
                on change { setLocale(chosen) }
                Select.Option("English", value: "en")
                Select.Option("العربية", value: "ar")
                Select.Option("Español", value: "es")
            }
        }
    }
    Router
}

page Home(path: "/", title: "Home", description: "The home page.") {
    Heading("Home").h1
    Text("Locale: {locale}, direction: {dir}")
    if locale == "ar" { Text("النسخة العربية") }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`locale` is the current locale's code, and `dir` is `\"ltr\"` or `\"rtl\"`. An `app` block may declare `state` as a page does, as `chosen` is here.\n\nOn a switch, the runtime sets `<html lang>` and `<html dir>`. A locale is right to left when its language is: Arabic, Hebrew, Persian, Urdu, Pashto, Sindhi, Central Kurdish, Uyghur, Yiddish, Dhivehi, Kashmiri, Syriac, Aramaic and N'Ko — with any region, so `ar-EG` is too. A site whose default locale is one of them is written `<html lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">` from the start, before any script runs.\n\n`meta.lang` may name a region of the default locale's language (`en-GB` for `en`); otherwise the default locale is the page's language.\n\nThe built-ins use logical CSS properties (`margin-inline-start`, not `margin-left`), so their layouts mirror with no work on your side. Use logical properties in your own `style \{ \}` blocks too.")

        Heading("Choose the locale a page opens in", id: "choose-the-locale-a-page-opens-in").h2

        Markdown("A page opens in `default_locale`, with two exceptions:\n\n1. **The address names a locale.** `?lang=<code>`, for one of the configured locales, opens the page in it: `/about?lang=ar` is the Arabic page, for a link you share. It does not change the reader's kept choice. It is not an address a search engine indexes: its HTML, and its canonical link, are the default locale's, so the build lists no `?lang=` address as an `hreflang` alternate. To have each language found, give it pages of its own ([A page in its own language](#a-page-in-its-own-language)).\n2. **The reader chose a locale before.** `setLocale(code)` keeps the code in the reader's `localStorage`, under `wf:locale`, and every page loaded after that opens in it, on this visit and the next. Choosing `default_locale` again forgets it.\n\nSo a static site, which loads each page afresh, keeps the reader's language from page to page, and a language switch can be a pair of buttons:")

        CodeBlock(#"app {
    Navbar {
        Navbar.Brand { Link("i18n", to: "/") }
        Navbar.Actions {
            Button("English") { on click { setLocale("en") } }
            Button("العربية") { on click { setLocale("ar") } }
        }
    }
    Router
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The runtime does not guess from the browser's language. Nothing is kept until the reader chooses, so a first visit gets the `?lang=` locale or the default, and a search engine's crawler the default. To start a first visit in the browser's language, keep whether the reader has chosen in a store with `persist`, and until they have, call `setLocale(navigator.language.slice(0, 2))` from an `effect` in the app.\n\nA static build pre-renders every page once, in the default locale; another locale is applied as the page loads. So a search engine indexes only the default locale's text. For each language to be found, give it pages of its own ([A page in its own language](#a-page-in-its-own-language)).")

        Heading("A page in its own language", id: "a-page-in-its-own-language").h2

        Markdown("`t()` and `setLocale` translate a page in the browser, at one address. A search engine indexes what that address's HTML says, which is the default locale. A site that wants each language found writes each page once per language, at an address of its own, and says so:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Home(path: "/", title: "منذر عمر", description: "مهندس خلفيات.") {
    Heading("مرحباً").h1
}

page HomeEn(path: "/en", title: "Monzer Omer", description: "Engineer.",
            lang: "en", translates: Home) {
    Heading("Hello").h1
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- **`lang:`** is the language the page is written in. A page without one is in the site's (`meta.lang`, or `default_locale`). The page is written `<html lang=\"en\">`, with `dir=\"rtl\"` for a language written right to left, and its `og:locale` and its JSON-LD's `inLanguage` say the same. The router sets `lang` and `dir` on `<html>` when it shows the page, and puts back the site's (or the locale the reader chose) on a page without one.\n- **`translates:`** names the page this one is a translation of. Pages joined this way — `HomeFr` may translate `Home` too — each list all of them as `hreflang` alternates, at their own addresses and canonical to themselves, with `x-default` for the one in the site's language, and each names the others' languages as `og:locale:alternate`.\n- A `:param` page's translation is matched by its parameters: `/en/posts/:slug` for `/posts/:slug`, filled with the same `slug`.\n\nA `translates:` that names no page, or two translations of one page in one language, is `R05`.")

        Heading("Format numbers and dates for the locale", id: "format-numbers-and-dates-for-the-locale").h2

        Markdown("`format` and `ago` follow the current locale ([Expressions](/docs/guide/expressions)). `format(1234.5, .currency, \"EUR\")` is `€1,234.50` in `en` and `1.234,50 €` in `de`; `ago(date)` says \"il y a 3 jours\" in `fr`. The pre-rendered page uses a table of the common locales' separators and currency symbols, and the live page uses the browser's `Intl` and corrects any difference.\n\nTo choose the digits a locale writes, add the Unicode extension to its code:\n\n- `setLocale(\"ar-u-nu-arab\")` reads the `ar` table and writes `format` — a date pattern too, its AM/PM in the locale's words — `ago` and every count in Arabic-Indic digits (١١ ملفًا).\n- `setLocale(\"ar-u-nu-latn\")` writes them in 0–9, whatever the browser's Arabic would pick.\n- Bare `ar` leaves the digits to the browser.\n\nA count is prose, so it takes the locale's digits. Another number spliced into a message — a line number, a version — stays as it is. The document's `lang` is the language, `ar`, whatever digits the code names.")

        Heading("Translate long-form text", id: "translate-long-form-text").h2

        Markdown("For a paragraph or a page of prose:\n\n- Under `\"literals\": true`, `Markdown(\"…\")` written in place is words like any other text: its whole source, `**` and all, is the key.\n- Text inside a `.md` page is not passed through `t`. Keep one Markdown file per locale, each with its own `path`, `lang` and `translates` in its front matter, or keep the text in the translation file under a key and render `Markdown(t(\"about.body\"))`.")

        Heading("Check what each locale lacks", id: "check-what-each-locale-lacks").h2

        Markdown("To see the translations as a table, run `wf translations`:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf translations"),
                Line(command: "wf translations --missing ar"),
                Line(command: "wf translations --unused"),
                Line(command: "wf translations --plurals")
            ])

        Table(caption: "Check what each locale lacks") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Command").header
                    Table.Cell("What it prints").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`wf translations`") }
                    Table.Cell("Each locale, the plural forms its rules use, and how many keys it lacks, a form it lacks, or a placeholder that differs from the default locale's")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`--missing ar`") }
                    Table.Cell("What Arabic lacks")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`--unused`") }
                    Table.Cell("The keys nothing names")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`--plurals`") }
                    Table.Cell("CLDR's plural rules: every locale's forms")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`--json`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The whole table: each key with its message in every locale, and every place a `t()` names it") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("From Rust the table is `webfluent::i18n::table` (with the `internals` feature, outside semver), which can also write one message back into its file with every other byte as it was.")

        Heading("Checklist", id: "checklist").h2

        Markdown("- Every visible string goes through `t`, including `label:`, `placeholder:` and `aria-label:` values.\n- No sentence is built from parts (`t(\"x\") + name`); a placeholder lets a translator reorder it.\n- Every count has its plural forms, even in English, and a `zero` where \"none\" reads better as a sentence.\n- `wf translations --missing <locale>` lists nothing for any locale.\n- You have tried the site once in a right-to-left locale, and checked:\n  - alignment, and icons that imply a direction (arrows);\n  - any `left` or `right` in your own CSS;\n  - a Latin code or figure inside Arabic text, which needs a left-to-right run of its own (`direction: ltr; unicode-bidi: isolate`) to keep its parts in order.\n- If each language should be found by search, each has pages of its own, with `lang:` and `translates:`, and `wf check` reports no `R05`.")
    }
}
